ary, in every group position | `error_paths.rs::e7_range_boundaries_in_all_positions` |","-| oversized length (2 GiB input, `int` overflow) | `alloc_failure.rs::e3b_int_overflow_makes_calloc_fail` |","-| out-of-range enum values across the FFI boundary | **N/A** — the public ABI has no `enum`, no flags and no integer parameters at all (single `const char *` parameter, `char *` return). The equivalent \"any bit pattern is a legal input\" surface is the byte domain, covered exhaustively by `e6_all_single_bytes` and `e7_range_boundaries_in_all_positions`. |","+`RLIMIT_DATA` on the verification host is 6 GiB, so a 2 GiB-scale allocation","+cannot succeed while a 2 GiB input string is held. Both implementations are","+subject to the identical limit — they are loaded into the *same* process — so the","+differential comparison stays valid; it simply means that for `strlen` near","+`INT_MAX` the C returns `NULL` from row 3/3b *or* row 4 depending on the exact","+length, and the Rust does the same in every case (verified for","+`strlen ∈ {INT_MAX-14 … INT_MAX}`)."]}],"originalFile":"# ERRORS.md — error-surface table (Phase A / Phase C)\n\nMechanically derived from every rejection point in `c_src/src/lib.c`.\nThere are no `assert`s, no error enums, no `errno` use and no range checks other\nthan the ones below; the *only* failure sentinel in the whole library is a\n`NULL` return from `decode_base64` (`lib.c:42` — \"Returns NULL in case of\nerror\"). Every `return (NULL);` statement in the C is one row.\n\n| # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test | status |\n|---|----------|---------------------------------------------|-------------------|------|--------|\n| 1 | `decode_base64` | `src == NULL` (`lib.c:46` `if (src && *src)` — first conjunct false) → falls through to `lib.c:112` | returns `NULL`; no allocation | `error_paths.rs::e1_null_pointer` | [x] |\n| 2 | `decode_base64` | `src != NULL` but `*src == '\\0'` (empty string, `lib.c:46` second conjunct false) → `lib.c:112` | returns `NULL`; no allocation | `error_paths.rs::e2_empty_string` | [x] |\n| 3 | `decode_base64` | `calloc(sizeof(char), l + 13)` returns `NULL` (`lib.c:53-56`), i.e. the destination allocation of `strlen(src) + 14` bytes fails because the process cannot supply that much memory | returns `NULL` (nothing to free) | `alloc_failure.rs::e3_calloc_fails_under_rlimit` | [x] |\n| 3b | `decode_base64` | same `calloc` check, reached via integer overflow of `int l = strlen(src) + 1` (`lib.c:49`): for `strlen(src) >= INT_MAX - 12` the `int` expression `l + 13` is negative, so the implicit conversion to `size_t` yields a huge value and `calloc` fails | returns `NULL` | `alloc_failure.rs::e3b_int_overflow_makes_calloc_fail` | [x] |\n| 4 | `decode_base64` | `malloc(l)` returns `NULL` *after* the `calloc` succeeded (`lib.c:60-64`): the scratch buffer allocation of `strlen(src) + 1` bytes fails | `free(dest)` then returns `NULL` (no leak of `dest`) | `alloc_failure.rs::e4_malloc_fails_after_calloc_succeeds` | [x] |\n\n## Non-rejections (deliberately *not* errors — verified as valid paths instead)\n\nThese look like error conditions but the C accepts them; they are covered by\n`CONFIGS.md` (Phase B), and asserting an error here would be wrong:\n\n| condition | C behaviour |\n|-----------|-------------|\n| bytes that are not in the base64 alphabet (incl. `0x80..0xFF`, whitespace, control chars) | silently skipped, \"Ignore non base64 chars as per the POSIX standard\" (`lib.c:66-71`) |\n| a string made *only* of non-alphabet bytes (`l == 0` after filtering) | decode loop body never runs; returns a **non-NULL**, zero-length (`calloc`-zeroed) buffer |\n| input length not a multiple of 4 / missing padding | missing positions default to `'A'` (`lib.c:74`, `79-89`) and are decoded |\n| `'='` anywhere (start, middle, repeated), more than two `'='` | never rejected; `decode('=') == 63`; only `c3`/`c4` (`lib.c:98`, `102`) suppress byte emission |\n| `'-'`, `'_'` (URL-safe alphabet) | not in `is_base64` → skipped, not an error |\n| decoded payload containing NUL bytes | written verbatim; buffer is `calloc`-zeroed so the *C string* stops early, the buffer keeps the rest |\n\n## Generic FFI boundary cases also covered (Phase C requirement)\n\n| case | test |\n|------|------|\n| `NULL` pointer | `e1_null_pointer` |\n| zero length (`\"\"`) | `e2_empty_string` |\n| pointer to a 1-byte buffer holding only NUL | `e2_empty_string` |\n| interior NUL (length \"lies\") — must stop at the first NUL | `error_paths.rs::e5_interior_nul_truncates` |\n| every single-byte input `0x01..=0xFF` (each byte one step in/out of every accepted range: `'A'-1`,`'Z'+1`,`'a'-1`,`'z'+1`,`'0'-1`,`'9'+1`,`'+'`,`'/'`,`'='`, DEL, high bytes) | `error_paths.rs::e6_all_single_bytes` |\n| values one step past every documented range boundary, in every group position | `error_paths.rs::e7_range_boundaries_in_all_positions` |\n| oversized length (2 GiB input, `int` overflow) | `alloc_failure.rs::e3b_int_overflow_makes_calloc_fail` |\n| out-of-range enum values across the FFI boundary | **N/A** — the public ABI has no `enum`, no flags and no integer parameters at all (single `const char *` parameter, `char *` return). The equivalent \"any bit pattern is a legal input\" surface is the byte domain, covered exhaustively by `e6_all_single_bytes` and `e7_range_boundaries_in_all_positions`. |\n","userModified":false}}
