{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_p4jejp5s3ofuakuwvuqmex2o7loo44vwmlwlgkeeqa3ogijs7boa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four phases are complete. Here's the final state.\n\n## Result: the Rust translation needed **no fixes** — it is byte-identical to the C on every input I could construct\n\n`src/lib.rs` is unmodified from the translation (verified by `diff`). I could not find a single divergence, and I confirmed the test suite is capable of finding one (see mutation study below).\n\n### Phase A — artifacts (all derived mechanically from the C)\n| file | content |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | The C `.so` exports exactly 1 symbol (`decode_base64`); `static` helpers `decode`/`is_base64` have internal linkage. Rust `.so` matches, symbol diff empty. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 6 rows — one per `return (NULL)` in `lib.c` (null src, empty string, `calloc` fails, `calloc` fails via `int` overflow, `malloc` fails after `calloc` succeeds) plus the one reachable input where the **C itself is undefined**. Plus generic FFI-boundary rows. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 25 rows. The library has no options/flags/`#ifdef`s, so the axes are input shape: `l mod 4`, group count, padding position, `decode` class per slot, non-alphabet bytes (incl. negative `char`), length sweeps, payload NULs, interior NUL. |\n| `CONFIGS.md` feature matrix | `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]` ⇒ exactly one combination (empty set), enumerated by script, `cargo check --all-targets` clean. |\n\n### Phases B/C/D — 40 tests in 6 binaries, all passing\nAll tests `dlopen` **both** `.so`s and compare **the entire `strlen+14` allocation** byte-for-byte (not `strcmp` — that would hide short/extra writes past the terminator), with fixed-seed randomization plus exhaustive sweeps (all 255 single bytes, all 65² pairs, all 65³ = 274,625 alphabet triples).\n\nVerified under: **dev + release** profiles × **C at `-O0`/`-O1`/`-O2`/`-O3`** (the `l + 13` signed overflow is UB, so gcc could legally diverge — it doesn't).\n\n### Three findings worth flagging\n1. **The C has UB on a reachable input**: `strlen == 4294967295` truncates `l` to `0`, so *both* NULL checks pass (`calloc(1,13)`, `malloc(0)`) and the filter loop writes 4 GiB into a 0-byte buffer. Both implementations die with **the same signal (SIGSEGV)** — tested in forked children (`ub_l_zero.rs`).\n2. **A naive `malloc_usable_size` equality check gives false positives** — glibc may serve a request from a larger free chunk, so the usable size depends on heap history. Replaced with a sound lower bound + exact equality only in the mmap regime.\n3. **In release builds LLVM deletes dead `malloc` calls** and folds the null check to \"not null\" — my address-space-exhaustion loop \"allocated\" 3.9 TiB while vsize never grew, i.e. the starvation was fake and the alloc-failure test would have been vacuous. Fixed with `black_box`; the child now *proves* which allocation fails via probes before calling the library.\n\n### Mutation study (proof the suite isn't vacuous)\n**23/23 semantic mutations detected**; **6/6 semantics-preserving controls correctly pass** — including subtle ones I initially misclassified as bugs (`b3 & 0x7` differs only in a bit that `as u8` truncates; `malloc(l-1)` is exactly big enough since the filter loop writes at most `strlen == l-1` bytes — `malloc(l-2)` *is* detected).\n\nTwo mutations are **environment-equivalent** and documented as such: they only differ when a ≥2 GiB allocation *succeeds*, which this host's 6 GiB `RLIMIT_DATA` forbids — so no input can distinguish them here.\n\nRun everything with `./verify.sh` (~100 s) or `./verify.sh --release`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":505,"cache_read_input_tokens":232586,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":505,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c199c1a9-b8ce-4fd8-b611-969766e92ee3","uuid":"9ee093a3-4d1c-4005-aab8-5695a011a324","timestamp":"2026-08-20T08:57:39.648Z"}
